t, which did happen (the near-miss happened). So 已发生 is correct for \"the board almost fell into the mud.\"\n\nLet me also check: is \"沈北川\" a character we can identify as the protagonist? The text just calls him 沈北川. We don't need to identify his role. We just record facts about him.\n\nFor fact 9, should I add \"沈北川\" to the fact? The text uses \"裤脚\" without explicitly saying whose, but from context (他 = 沈北川), it's clearly his. The evidence is \"泥水渗进裤脚\" - this clearly refers to the subject of the previous sentence (他 = 沈北川). I think it's clear enough. But to be safe, I could say \"泥水渗进沈北川裤脚\" as the fact, with the evidence showing the contextual pronoun.\n\nActually, looking at the rules more carefully: \"不得用常识、原著记忆或背景卡补出当前章节没有表达的事件。\" But this isn't adding an unstated event - it's just clarifying whose trouser legs. The pronoun \"他\" in the previous sentence refers to 沈北川. I think it's fine.\n\nLet me finalize the JSON output.\n\nOne more consideration: the message \"货已经卸完\" - is this something I need to record as a fact? The message itself is speech content. Per the rules: \"人物已经说出口的询问、请求、命令、劝说、建议和评价，本身都按已经发生的言语事件记录；说话内容不能因此升级成客观事实。\" The message is a statement/announcement (not a question, request, command, persuasion, suggestion, or evaluation per se, but it is a speech event). The fact is the speech event - that a message was transmitted. The content \"货已经卸完\" (goods have been unloaded) - should I record this as an objective fact? \n\nThe rules say \"说话内容不能因此升级成客观事实\" - so the content of the message should not be promoted to an objective fact just because it was spoken. However, in the context of a walkie-talkie message in a novel, this is often treated as information. But strictly following the rules, I should record:\n- The speech event: 对讲机传来消息\"货已经卸完\"\n- Not upgrade the content to an objective fact\n\nBut wait, the content of the message could be a 推测 from the speaker (the person at the other end of the walkie-talkie). Or it could be treated as reporte